# Introduction
This week’s project extends the Hidden Markov Model Viterbi implementation from last week by adding the Forward algorithm, 
Backward algorithm, and Forward-Backward algorithm within the same object-oriented class structure. While the Viterbi 
algorithm computes the overall optimal hidden state sequence, the Forward-Backward algorithm computes the posterior 
probability of being in each hidden state at every position in the observation sequence, given the full observation.

The Forward algorithm fills a matrix of shape (n_states x length of observation) from left to right. The first column 
is filled using the initial state probabilities and the emission probability of the first observed character. Each cell 
in the matrix is computed by summing over all previous states, combining the previous forward score, the transition 
log-probability into the current state, and the emission log-probability of the current observed character, 
using np.logaddexp.reduce to sum in log space.

The Backward algorithm does the same computation as the Forward algorithm but traverses the observation sequence from 
right to left. To implement this cleanly, the observation sequence is reversed, and the backward matrix is filled left 
to right from the reversed sequence, then flipped back to the original column order at the end. Both forward and backward
algorithm matrices are called upon to complete the Forward-Backward approach, which yields a more robust probability
result for each hidden state in the observation sequence since the calculation is taking into account the probabilities
generated from both directions.

The Forward-Backward algorithm combines both the forward and backward matrices to compute the posterior probability of each 
state at each position in the final forward-backward matrix. For each cell (i, j) in the forward backward matrix, the posterior 
is computed as the sum of the log- forward score and log- backward score at that position, minus the total log-probability of the 
observation sequence. The total log-probability is obtained by applying np.logaddexp.reduce to the last column of either the forward 
matrix or backwards matrix.

A key addition to our object-oriented approach this week is the StrMatrix class, which handles the conversion of nested 
probability dictionaries into NumPy matrices while preserving the key-to-index mapping. In the original dictionary structure 
of the emission dictionary, the outer key specifies the row, and the inner key specifies the column. StrMatrix constructs two 
encoding dictionaries: outer_key_map and inner_key_map, that map each string key to its corresponding integer index. This allows 
the probability matrices to be stored as pure NumPy arrays and indexed by integer position at runtime, avoiding string-based 
dictionary lookups inside the loops of the dynamic programming algorithms. The transition and emission probability tables are 
both converted through StrMatrix at initialisation, and the resulting matrix objects are stored as self.trans_matrix and 
self.emit_matrix respectively. 

All probability calculations are performed in natural log space for the same reasons as the Viterbi algorithm- to avoid 
numerical underflow from repeated multiplication of small decimal probabilities. In log space, summing probabilities 
across multiple paths requires the logaddexp operation rather than standard summation.
  


# Pseudocode

```python

function fwd:
    initialize zeros matrix of size n_states x observations
    
    find first_col probabilities
    
    for col in remaining cols:
        for state in states:
            matrix[row][col] = log_sum_exp(prev_score[row][prev_col] * transition_matrix[state][row] * emission_matrix[state][observation])
    
    return matrix, log_sum_exp(matrix[last_column])


function bwd:
    initialize zeros matrix of size n_states x observations
    reverse observations
    
    set first col to 0 (which is a probability of 1)
    
    for col in remaining cols: # same loop as the first time except everything is backwards and the start is 1
        for state in states:
            matrix[row][col] = log_sum_exp(prev_score[row][prev_col] * transition_matrix[state][row] * emission_matrix[state][observation])
    
    return matrix

function fwd-bwd:

    FWD, P_seq = fwd
    BWD = bwd

    G = (FWD * BWD) / P_seq

    return states associated with argmax of each column

```

# Successes
We successfully converted the input data structure from dictionary to a matrix, so it is easier to get the probabilities 
and broadcast them instead of looping inside a dictionary whenever we get the probabilities for calculations.

# Struggles
Expanding last week’s HMMModel into a full multi‑algorithm implementation turned out to be far more difficult than expected 
because the original design simply wasn’t built to support anything beyond Viterbi. The earlier version mixed state labels, 
probability lookups, and dynamic‑programming logic in a way that worked for a single algorithm but collapsed the moment we 
tried to add Forward, Backward, and Forward–Backward. The DP matrix was created manually inside Viterbi with hard‑coded shapes 
and mixed data types, so every new method would have needed to re‑implement its own version of the same logic, guaranteeing 
duplication and inconsistent behavior. The probability structures were stored as nested dictionaries, which made vectorized 
operations impossible and forced slow, deeply nested loops that were incompatible with the mathematical structure of the 
Forward and Backward recursions. Even the traceback logic was tightly coupled to Viterbi’s DP layout, which meant the class 
had no clean separation between probability storage, matrix initialization, and algorithm‑specific behavior. As soon as we 
tried to extend the class, these structural limitations surfaced immediately and repeatedly.

The first attempts to “patch in” Forward and Backward made this even clearer. Each algorithm needed its own initialization rules, 
recursion rules, and termination rules, but the class had no shared mechanism for allocating a clean numeric matrix. The original 
Viterbi matrix even stored headers inside the matrix itself, which made it unusable for any algorithm that required pure numeric 
values. The dictionary‑based probability storage also became a major bottleneck, because Forward and Backward require fast 
column‑wise access and consistent log‑space arithmetic, neither of which is feasible with nested dicts. Every attempt to extend 
the class resulted in duplicated code, shape mismatches, and inconsistent behavior across algorithms. It became obvious that the 
only viable path forward was a structural rewrite rather than incremental patching.

The turning point was introducing a dedicated matrix‑initialization method that every algorithm could rely on. By centralizing the 
creation of DP matrices through a single initialise_matrix function, we eliminated the repeated shape logic, enforced consistent 
log‑space initialization, and ensured that all algorithms operated on clean, numeric matrices without embedded labels. This also 
allowed us to modularize the probability structures by moving all preprocessing into a separate module containing the 
dictionary‑maker and the string‑to‑matrix converter. That module standardized the ordering of states and symbols, converted all 
probabilities into log‑space matrices, and provided the fast, vectorized access patterns required by Forward, Backward, and 
Forward–Backward. Once these pieces were in place, the algorithms themselves became dramatically cleaner, more consistent, and 
easier to maintain.

Even after the algorithms were correct, we continued to struggle with output formatting, especially when trying to prepare the 
notebook for GitHub. NumPy object arrays always display their internal representation (np.str_('G')) when printed directly, even 
when the underlying values are plain Python strings, which made the traceback matrices look messy and vertical. Jupyter’s HTML 
rendering made the matrices look clean inside the notebook, but GitHub strips HTML and collapses whitespace, so the beautifully 
formatted tables we saw locally were reduced to raw arrays in the repository. We also ran into persistent issues with stale variables 
and out‑of‑order cell execution, which made debugging formatting problems harder than debugging the algorithms themselves. By the time 
the algorithms, matrices, and module structure were fully correct, we simply ran out of time to implement a GitHub‑friendly fallback 
renderer or fully resolve the display inconsistencies. The core computational work was completed successfully, but the formatting layer 
remained partially unresolved due to time constraints and the inherent limitations of how GitHub renders Jupyter output.
 

